{"name":"Golden Lion Tamarin","hex":"#ca602a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden lion tamarin","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A monkey of species Leontopithecus rosalia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden_lion_tamarin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golden lion tamarin","blocks":[{"text":"The golden lion tamarin, less commonly known as the golden lion marmoset, is a small New World monkey of the family Callitrichidae. Endemic to the Atlantic coastal forests of Brazil, the golden lion tamarin is an endangered species. Its geographic range is entirely within the state of Rio de Janeiro. A 2022/2023 census estimated about 4,800 individuals living in the current primary area of occurrence in the non-coastal area of the São João and Macaé river basins, with unknown but smaller additional numbers in limited coastal forests and to the west of the primary area of occurrence. There is a captive population maintaining about 490 individuals among 150 zoos."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Leontopithecus_rosalia_-_Copenhagen_Zoo_-_DSC09082.JPG/330px-Leontopithecus_rosalia_-_Copenhagen_Zoo_-_DSC09082.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_lion_tamarin","wikidata":"Q208486"},{"title":"Lion tamarin","blocks":[{"text":"The four species of lion tamarins or maned marmosets make up the genus Leontopithecus, from Ancient Greek λέων (léōn), meaning \"lion\", and πίθηκος (píthēkos), meaning \"monkey\". They are small New World monkeys named for the mane surrounding their face, similar to the mane of a lion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Tamarin.monkey.500pix.jpg/330px-Tamarin.monkey.500pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion_tamarin","wikidata":"Q857486"},{"title":"Golden Lion","blocks":[{"text":"The Golden Lion is the highest prize given to a film at the Venice Film Festival. The prize was introduced in 1949 by the organizing committee, as the successor to the earlier Gran Premio Internazionale di Venezia, awarded in 1947 and 1948. The prize was introduced as the Golden Golden Lion of Saint Mark, which was one of the best known symbols of the ancient Republic of Venice. In 1954, the prize was permanently named the Golden Lion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/May_el-Toukhy_with_the_Golden_Lion_for_%22Woman_Unknown%22_at_Venice_Film_Festival_83_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-May_el-Toukhy_with_the_Golden_Lion_for_%22Woman_Unknown%22_at_Venice_Film_Festival_83_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Lion","wikidata":"Q209459"},{"title":"Lion","blocks":[{"text":"The lion is a large cat of the genus Panthera, currently ranging only in Sub-Saharan Africa and India. It has a muscular, broad-chested body; a short, rounded head; round ears; and a dark, hairy tuft at the tip of its tail. It is sexually dimorphic; adult male lions are larger than females and have a prominent mane that extends from the head to the shoulders and chest."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/330px-020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion","wikidata":"Q140"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Stavenn_Leontopithecus_rosalia_01.jpg/330px-Stavenn_Leontopithecus_rosalia_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Stavenn_Leontopithecus_rosalia_01.jpg/330px-Stavenn_Leontopithecus_rosalia_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStavenn_Leontopithecus_rosalia_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Golden_Lion_Tamarin_Leontopithecus_rosalia.jpg/500px-Golden_Lion_Tamarin_Leontopithecus_rosalia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Golden_Lion_Tamarin_Leontopithecus_rosalia.jpg/500px-Golden_Lion_Tamarin_Leontopithecus_rosalia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Closeup on the golden lion tamarin's face","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGolden_Lion_Tamarin_Leontopithecus_rosalia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Golden_lion_tamarin_family.jpg/500px-Golden_lion_tamarin_family.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Golden_lion_tamarin_family.jpg/500px-Golden_lion_tamarin_family.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Family groups may consist of up to eight members.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGolden_lion_tamarin_family.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Golden_Lion_Tamarin_and_Baby.jpg/500px-Golden_Lion_Tamarin_and_Baby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Golden_Lion_Tamarin_and_Baby.jpg/500px-Golden_Lion_Tamarin_and_Baby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The mother provides transportation at the early stages of the infant's life.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGolden_Lion_Tamarin_and_Baby.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/th